# There is no server at the other end of this build, so this file is the whole
# of what the site can say about itself in a header. Everything here is set on
# `/*` and every rule below adds to it -- Pages applies each matching block, and
# the four type rules name a header this one does not.
#
# The one directive that is weaker than it looks is `script-src`. A static
# export cannot carry a nonce, since there is no request to mint one on, and it
# cannot carry a hash list either: the fourteen inline scripts in a built page
# are mostly Next's own RSC payload, whose contents change with the page and
# with every build. A hash list would have to be regenerated into this file
# after each build or the site would refuse to run itself. So CSP is not the
# thing standing between this site and injected script -- that is `default-src
# 'none'` and the four directives under it, which mean an injected script can
# still not reach a third party, rewrite the base URL, post a form, or be framed
# by anyone.
#
# Everything the built pages load is same-origin: the chunks and the CSS under
# `/_next/static`, the two vendored woff2 files, one jpg and the icon. There is
# no form, no iframe, no third-party host, and no runtime fetch anywhere on the
# site, which is why `connect-src 'self'` costs nothing.
#
# `Strict-Transport-Security` deliberately carries neither `includeSubDomains`
# nor `preload`. Both are close to irreversible for the length of the max-age,
# and a subdomain added later and served over plain HTTP would be unreachable
# with no obvious reason why. Add them when there is a subdomain to check.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000

# Cloudflare Pages types a file by its extension, and the four routes below are
# the only files in the build that do not have one: Next names a metadata image
# after its route, so they are written as `out/icon` and `out/opengraph-image`.
# Untyped they go out as application/octet-stream, which is a favicon a browser
# will not draw and a card no scraper will open.
#
# Nothing else needs a type line. Every other file in `out/` is a `.html`, a
# `.png`, a `.txt` or a `.xml` and says so in its name.
#
# `:slug` matches one path segment, which is the shape of the post cards. A
# splat would be the wrong tool: it is greedy, and `/blog/*` is the blog itself.

/icon
  Content-Type: image/png

/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png

/blog/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png

/blog/:slug/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png
